/* A class whose initializer throws: what the program reports, and that it is
 * reported as the JDK reports it.
 *
 * Java wraps an initializer's failure in ExceptionInInitializerError (JLS
 * 12.4.2) unless the throwable is an Error, which is rethrown as it is. This
 * program's initializer throws a RuntimeException, so the first line of the
 * report is `java.lang.ExceptionInInitializerError` -- and that is what the
 * JDK prints for the same program, measured:
 *
 *   Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ExceptionInInitializerError
 *   Caused by: java.lang.RuntimeException: init failed
 *       at Boom.boom(Boom.java:4)
 *       at Boom.<clinit>(Boom.java:2)
 *
 * This compiler prints the first line and no stack trace, which is the same
 * difference every uncaught report here has (t79, t80); what this pins is that
 * the first line is the JDK's, where it used to be the raw
 * `java.lang.RuntimeException: init failed`.
 *
 * The class is a program class, and the compiler runs a program's own classes
 * before main (AGENTS.md §10 records that as this compiler's order rather than
 * JLS 12.4.1's, kept by the owner's decision), so the failure happens at startup
 * and main never runs. That is also why the second half of JLS 12.4.2 -- an
 * erroneous class refusing every later use with NoClassDefFoundError -- cannot
 * be reached from a program today: it needs a class the compiler leaves to its
 * first use, which is a standard-library class, and no library class has an
 * initializer that can throw. The runtime implements it anyway (tyrt2.c's
 * TY_CLS_ERR); this file is the half that is observable.
 *
 * The initializer is written to fail on a value the compiler cannot fold, so
 * that the failure is a run-time one: an initializer the checker rejects would
 * be a diagnostics case, not this one. */
class Boom {
  public static int x = boom()

  static int boom() {
    String s = "init " + "failed"
    if (s.length() > 0) {
      throw new RuntimeException(s)
    }
    return 1
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println("never")
  }
}
